---
<Layout title="Work" description="An atlas of platforms, identity systems, and the decisions behind them. Engineering work by Viswaroop Vadlamudi.">
<div class="atlas-topline"><a href="/">PERSONAL ATLAS</a><span>01 / SYSTEMS</span></div>
<header class="work-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow orange">Built, operated, reconsidered.</p><h1>UNDER THE<br/>SURFACE<span class="orange">.</span></h1></div><p>A system diagram shows the connections.<br/><br/>These are the decisions underneath them.</p></header>
<nav class="project-index" aria-label="Jump to a system"><a href="#multi-cloud"><span>01</span> Delivery across clouds ↘</a><a href="#identity"><span>02</span> Owning identity ↘</a><a href="#reliability"><span>03</span> Reliability loops ↘</a><a href="#agents"><span>04</span> Agent boundaries ↘</a></nav>
<div class="page-body">
<section class="dossier" id="multi-cloud"><header class="dossier-head"><span class="dossier-number">01</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Platform engineering / Architecture & implementation</p><h2>Different clouds.<br/>One way to ship.</h2></div><span class="dossier-code">SYS—001</span></header><SystemPlate kind="platform"/><div class="plate-caption"><span>FIG. 01 — DELIVERY CONTRACT</span><span>Argo CD / Kustomize / Helm / EKS / GKE</span></div><div class="decision-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">The friction</p><p>More teams meant more deployment scripts, console changes, and coordination. The platform needed a delivery path that stayed understandable as it grew.</p></div><div><p class="eyebrow">My contribution</p><p>I designed a multi-tenant GitOps platform across EKS and GKE, with tenant quotas, network policies, promotion pipelines, and operational readiness in the templates.</p></div><div class="decision-note"><p class="eyebrow">The decision that matters</p><p>Standardize the delivery contract. Keep the cloud-specific tradeoffs visible.</p></div></div><details class="implementation"><summary>Inside the implementation <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="prose"><ul><li>Tenant-aware namespaces with resource quotas and network policies.</li><li>Promotion pipelines that account for blast radius before production.</li><li>Logging, metrics, and SLO definitions embedded in deployment templates.</li></ul><p>The operational differences between clouds still matter. A consistent delivery path should make those differences explicit, rather than pretend they don’t exist.</p></div></details><a class="dossier-related" href="/blog/2025-12-22-argo-cd-vs-flux-in-production/"><span>CONNECTED NOTE</span> Argo CD vs Flux in production <b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a></section>
<section class="dossier" id="identity"><header class="dossier-head"><span class="dossier-number">02</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Identity & tenancy / Architecture & migration</p><h2>Own the boundary.<br/>Not just the login.</h2></div><span class="dossier-code">SYS—002</span></header><SystemPlate kind="identity"/><div class="plate-caption"><span>FIG. 02 — SEPARATE RESPONSIBILITIES</span><span>ORY Hydra / Kratos / Keto / OAuth2</span></div><div class="decision-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">The friction</p><p>B2B multi-tenancy, enterprise SSO, and fine-grained permissions were pushing beyond the existing managed identity setup.</p></div><div><p class="eyebrow">My contribution</p><p>I led the architecture and migration from AWS Cognito to ORY, separating authentication, OAuth2 token flows, and relationship-based authorization.</p></div><div class="decision-note"><p class="eyebrow">The decision that matters</p><p>Make each boundary explicit, with clear ownership for the teams integrating with it.</p></div></div><details class="implementation"><summary>Inside the implementation <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="prose"><ul><li>Modeled tenant boundaries and token flows across services.</li><li>Built predictable self-service onboarding for application integrations.</li><li>Used Keto for relationship-based access control.</li></ul><p>Moving providers was one part of the work. Deciding where authentication ends, authorization begins, and product teams take responsibility was the more lasting part.</p></div></details><a class="dossier-related" href="/blog/2025-12-21-identity-platforms-fail-at-the-frontend-boundary-part-1-the-boundary-problem/"><span>CONNECTED NOTE</span> Identity fails at the frontend boundary <b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a></section>
<section class="dossier" id="reliability"><header class="dossier-head"><span class="dossier-number">03</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Reliability / Guardrails & operating practices</p><h2>Make the safe path<br/>the obvious one.</h2></div><span class="dossier-code">SYS—003</span></header><SystemPlate kind="reliability"/><div class="plate-caption"><span>FIG. 03 — CLOSE THE LEARNING LOOP</span><span>OPA / SLOs / GitOps / Runbooks / ADRs</span></div><div class="decision-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">The friction</p><p>Manual review gates created delays, while operational expectations stayed disconnected from the way teams shipped.</p></div><div><p class="eyebrow">My contribution</p><p>I embedded policy checks in CI, co-owned SLOs and incident runbooks with product teams, and recorded cost and reliability tradeoffs in ADRs.</p></div><div class="decision-note"><p class="eyebrow">The decision that matters</p><p>Put guardrails in the workflow. Put learning back into the next change.</p></div></div><a class="dossier-related" href="/blog/2026-01-10-observability-on-aws-at-scale-six-years-of-lessons-from-opensearch-eks-and-production-reality/"><span>CONNECTED NOTE</span> Six years of observability lessons <b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a></section>
<section class="dossier exploration" id="agents"><header class="dossier-head"><span class="dossier-number">04</span><div><p class="eyebrow orange">Ongoing exploration / Not a production case study</p><h2>What if the caller<br/>isn’t a person?</h2></div><span class="dossier-code">WIP—004</span></header><div class="exploration-intro"><p>I’m exploring how autonomous workflows should act on behalf of users, with scoped identity and isolated execution.</p><p class="question">What has to be true before an agent is allowed to act?</p></div><AgentSandboxDemo/><a class="dossier-related" href="/blog/2026-03-28-why-ai-agents-will-break-your-identity-platform/"><span>CONNECTED NOTE</span> Why agents will break your identity platform <b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a></section>
<section class="atlas-signoff"><p>Your system probably has<br/>an interesting boundary, too.</p><a href="/services/">LET’S LOOK AT IT <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
</div>
</Layout>
